analysisWe need to produce an assert statement for the comment in the constructor.

Comment: "If the list is not null, then for every valid index in this list, the element at that index is the same as the element at the same index in the original list."

We are in constructor AttributeList(AttributeList list). It calls super(list) which copies elements.

We need to assert that if list != null, then for all int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); this.get(i).equals(\old(list.get(i)))? Wait we need to refer to original list's elements before construction. Since list is a parameter, its state before call is same as after (no modification). Use \old(list) maybe not needed because list is not modified; but we can still use \old(list.get(i)). However the old value of list is same as current list. But spec says for every valid index in this list (the new list), the element at that index is the same as the element at the same index in the original list. So we need to express: list != null => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); this.get(i).equals(list.get(i));

But need to ensure we use observer methods: this.get(i) returns Attribute; list.get(i) also returns Attribute (list is AttributeList). So we can write this.get(i).equals(\old(list.get(i)))? Since list is not changed, old is same, but we could still use old. Might be safer to use \old(list.get(i)) to capture original state. Use \old(list.get(i)).

Thus final assert:

assert list != null => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); this.get(i).equals(\old(list.get(i)));

Make sure syntax: need parentheses for implication: (list != null) => (\forall ...). Actually implication operator => is binary, lower precedence than &&, so we need parentheses around left? Usually we write list != null => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); this.get(i).equals(\old(list.get(i)));

But to be safe: assert (list != null) => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); this.get(i).equals(\old(list.get(i)));

Thus final answer.

assistantfinal<code>assert (list != null) => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); this.get(i).equals(\old(list.get(i)));</code>
